            "result_text": "Perfect! The regex has been saved to `/app/regex.txt`.\n\n## Regex Breakdown\n\nThe pattern I created handles all your requirements:\n\n**Key Components:**\n\n1. **Start of line assertion** (`^`) with lookahead for IPv4: Ensures the line contains a valid IPv4 address with proper word boundaries `(?![a-zA-Z0-9])`\n\n2. **IPv4 pattern**: `(?:25[0-5]|2[0-4][0-9]|1[0-9]{2}|[1-9][0-9]|[0-9])` \n   - Matches 0-255 without leading zeros\n   - Each octet pattern ordered longest-first to avoid premature matches\n\n3. **Greedy match to last date**: `.*(?<![a-zA-Z0-9])` \n   - The greedy `.*` will match as much as possible while the lookbehind ensures the date isn't preceded by alphanumeric\n\n4. **Date capture**: `(\\d{4}-(0[1-9]|1[0-2])-(0[1-9]|[12][0-9]))`\n   - Year: any 4 digits\n   - Month: 01-12\n   - Day: 01-29 (allowing Feb 29 in all years as specified)\n\n5. **Word boundary with lookahead**: `(?![a-zA-Z0-9])` prevents matching if followed by alphanumeric\n\n6. **Last date guarantee**: The negative lookahead `(?!.*(?<![a-zA-Z0-9])\\d{4}-(0[1-9]|1[0-2])-(0[1-9]|[12][0-9])(?![a-zA-Z0-9]))` ensures no additional dates exist after the captured one\n\nThe regex correctly handles cases like:\n- Lines with multiple dates → returns only the last one\n- False dates like \"user1134-12-1234\" → skipped (day 1234 > 29)\n- Lines without IPv4 → skipped\n- Invalid IPv4 like \"192.168.1.256\" → skipped"
